ear":1990,"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"The University of Chicago Press"},"copyrightHolder":{"@type":"Organization","name":"The University of Chicago Press"},"author":[{"@type":"Person","name":"Joseph Connors"}],"abstract":"Ars Tornandi: Baroque Architecture and the Lathe is an article examining the lathe's role in Baroque architectural theory and practice. It traces the lathe’s ancient pedigree, its social ascent among European elites, and its influence on Baroque architecture, especially Borromini, through the lens of ornamental turning as described by Charles Plumier. The piece connects turning to architectural profiled mouldings, discusses architectura obliqua, and examines how imagination and genius challenged rules in architecture.","keywords":"[\"Baroque architecture\", \"lathe / turning\", \"ornamental turning\", \"Borromini\", \"architectura obliqua\", \"Plumier\", \"Michelangelo influence on architecture\", \"Balusters and mouldings\", \"Kunstkammer / instrumental culture\", \"architectural theory vs. rule\"]","about":[{"@type":"Thing","name":"Philosophy"},{"@type":"Thing","name":"Humanities"},{"@type":"Thing","name":"Art \u0026 Art History"},{"@type":"Thing","name":"Arts"},{"@type":"Thing","name":"History"},{"@type":"Thing","name":"History"}],"isPartOf":{"@type":"PublicationIssue","isPartOf":{"@type":"PublicationVolume","volumeNumber":"53","isPartOf":{"@type":"Periodical","name":"Journal of the Warburg and Courtauld Institutes","publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"The University of Chicago Press"},"identifier":[{"@type":"PropertyValue","propertyID":"issn","value":"0075-4390"}]}}}}</script><!----><!--]--><!----><!--1h0n4nl--><script type="text/javascript" data-analytics-provider="ga">
